```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(fig.path = "examples-figures/")
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
```

I present three examples to demonstrate how the MMMM can be applied to analyze aggregation, network, and spatial analyses.

-   [Example 1: All Friends or Just Your Best Friend?](#e1) (network regression)
-   [Example 2: Air Quality and Home Values](#e2) (spatial regression)
-   [Example 3: Political Parties and the Survival of Coalition Governments](#e3) (aggregation regression)

### Example 1: All friends, or just your best friend? {#e1}

In this example, our core question is: How does peer influence add up across a friendship group?
We look at how peers influence an individual's behavior in terms of smoking.

We use the CILS4EU Germany Wave 1 friendship network data, which covers classroom-level friendships in German secondary schools.
Students nominated up to five friends and ranked them by closeness (1 = best friend, 5 = fifth-closest).
We predict Wave 2 smoking from Wave 1 friends' smoking, using the one-year lag so a student's future behaviour cannot have caused her current friends' behaviour.

```{r, message = FALSE, warning = FALSE}
#| code-fold: true
library(arrow)
library(dplyr)
library(bml)

```
```{r, echo = FALSE, message = FALSE, warning = FALSE}
#| code-fold: true
.cils_dir  <- "/Users/komal.npn/bml/data"
nodedat    <- read_feather(file.path(.cils_dir, "nodedat.feather"))
edgedat    <- read_feather(file.path(.cils_dir, "edgedat.feather"))
nodedat_w2 <- read_feather(file.path(.cils_dir, "nodedat-w2.feather"))
```

```{r, message = FALSE, warning = FALSE}
#| code-fold: true

# Verify rank as an integer (1 = best friend, 5 = fifth-closest)
edgedat <- edgedat |>
  mutate(rank = as.integer(rank))

# Build long-format: one row per (ego, alter) pair
w2_outcome <- nodedat_w2 |> select(youthid, smoking_w2 = student_smoking)
ego_vars   <- nodedat    |> select(youthid, smoking_ego = student_smoking,
                                            gender_ego  = student_gender)
alter_vars <- nodedat    |> select(youthid, smoking_alter = student_smoking)

cils_long <-
  edgedat |>
  inner_join(ego_vars,   by = c("youthid_from" = "youthid")) |>
  inner_join(alter_vars, by = c("youthid_to"   = "youthid")) |>
  inner_join(w2_outcome, by = c("youthid_from" = "youthid")) |>
  group_by(youthid_from) |> mutate(n = n()) |> ungroup() |>
  rename(ego = youthid_from, alter = youthid_to)
```

In terms of modeling, we will be fitting two different models for demonstration and we will comparing them. 
The economics standard for this network analysis problem is the linear-in-means model: every friend carries equal weight, so what matters is the average across all of them. This would mean that every friend has equal influence on the smoking behavior of an individual. 
A contention to that assumption would be that not every friend has the same level of influence in our lives and the weight should be proportional to their effect. So the opposite our first assumption of equal weights would be that a single close friend drives most of the influence while looser ties matter little.
`bml` lets us fit both and compare them directly, by changing one line of the weight function.

#### Model 1: linear-in-means

Here, every friend gets equal weight, `w ~ 1/n`.
The peer term is the simple average of all nominated friends' Wave 1 smoking.

```{r}
mod_lim <-
  bml(
    smoking_w2 ~ smoking_ego + gender_ego +
      mm(
        id   = id(alter, ego),
        vars = bml::vars(smoking_alter),
        fn   = fn(w ~ 1/n, c = TRUE),
        RE   = TRUE
      ),
    family   = "Gaussian",
    n.iter   = 5000,
    n.burnin = 1000,
    n.chains = 3,
    seed     = 1,
    data     = cils_long
  )

summary(mod_lim)
```

#### Model 2: best friend only

Here, only the closest-ranked friend contributes and all other friends are not considered.
`rank == min(rank)` evaluates to `TRUE` for the closest-ranked friend within each ego's friend set (rank 1), putting all weight on that friend.
With `c = TRUE` the weight is renormalised to 1 within each ego, so the peer term becomes that single friend's smoking.

```{r}
mod_bf <-
  bml(
    smoking_w2 ~ smoking_ego + gender_ego +
      mm(
        id   = id(alter, ego),
        vars = bml::vars(smoking_alter),
        fn   = fn(w ~ rank == min(rank), c = TRUE),
        RE   = TRUE
      ),
    family   = "Gaussian",
    n.iter   = 5000,
    n.burnin = 1000,
    n.chains = 3,
    seed     = 1,
    data     = cils_long
  )

summary(mod_bf)
```

#### Comparing the two models

`bml` provides `tidy()` and `glance()` methods, so we can pull estimates and fit statistics into tidy tibbles and compare the models side by side.

```{r}
#| code-fold: true
# Peer effect and fit side by side
coef_comparison <- bind_rows(
  tidy(mod_lim) |> mutate(model = "Linear-in-means"),
  tidy(mod_bf)  |> mutate(model = "Best friend only")
) |>
  filter(grepl("smoking_alter", term)) |>
  select(model, estimate, conf.low, conf.high)

coef_comparison

bind_rows(
  glance(mod_lim) |> mutate(model = "Linear-in-means"),
  glance(mod_bf)  |> mutate(model = "Best friend only")
) |>
  select(model, DIC, nobs)
```

We can see that both models find a positive peer effect. It means that students whose friends smoke more tend to smoke more themselves one year later, over and above their own Wave 1 smoking.
The own-smoking coefficient (`smoking_ego` of 0.365 in the first model and 0.358 in the second one) dominates in both models, which is expected since past behaviour is the strongest predictor of future behaviour.

The peer effect is present but modest.
Under linear-in-means, averaging across all nominated friends gives a coefficient
of 0.04 (CI [0.00, 0.08]), which just clears zero.
Limiting to just the best friend gives 0.03 (CI [0.005, 0.06]), which is smaller in point estimate but has a tighter in interval  which can be attributed to the fact that the best-friend model discards the noisier lower-ranked ties.

Looking at the DIC, the best-friend model fits better (15,811 vs 16,866), a gap of over 1000. Focusing on the single closest friend is a better description of how peer influence on smoking aggregates than treating all friends as equally important.

### Example 2: Air quality and home values {#e2}

This **spatial-analysis** example is based on [Harrison & Rubinfeld (1978)](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0095069678900062), who study the effect of air quality on home values, and the re-analysis by [Bivand (2017)](https://openjournals.wu.ac.at/region/paper_107/107.html).

Example 1 asked how a person's friends combine to influence them.
This example asks a spatial version of the same aggregation question: how do the tracts(units of neighbours) around a neighbourhood combine to shape its home values? 

We use the 1970 Boston census tract data from Harrison and Rubinfeld (1978), treating each tract's outcome as a function of its own attributes and those of its neighbours.

The question we add to the classical modeling approach is whether it is beneficial when every neighbour counts equally, or when tracts weigh their neighbours by how similar they are.

#### Data and spatial weights

```{r, message = FALSE, warning = FALSE}
#| code-fold: true
library(spData)
library(sf)
library(spdep)
library(spatialreg)
library(ggplot2)
library(bml)

boston <-
  read_sf(system.file("shapes/boston_tracts.gpkg", package = "spData")) |>
  select(CMEDV, NOX, CRIM, RM, DIS, AGE, geom) |>
  st_transform(crs = 5070) |>
  mutate(
    tid     = row_number(),
    lnCMEDV = log(CMEDV),
    across(c(NOX, CRIM, RM, DIS, AGE), ~ as.numeric(scale(.)))
  )

boston_nb    <- poly2nb(as_Spatial(boston), row.names = boston$tid)
boston_wlist <- nb2listw(boston_nb, style = "W")
```

The data are the 1970 Boston Standard Metropolitan Statistical Area census tracts that ship with `spData`.
After dropping tracts with no housing and tracts that are entirely institutional, 506 tracts remain.
Each tract records its median home value, the local nitric-oxides concentration (the air-quality measure), and a handful of standard housing and demographic covariates.

We model `lnCMEDV`, the log of median home value, to tame the skew in raw prices, and we standardise every covariate so the coefficients are directly comparable.
The variables of our interest are:

| Variable  | Role      | Meaning                                                  |
|-----------|-----------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| `tid`     | id        | Tract id (1 to 506)                                      |
| `lnCMEDV` | outcome   | Log median home value, the quantity we model             |
| `NOX`     | predictor | Nitric-oxides concentration, the air-quality measure     |
| `CRIM`    | predictor | Per-capita crime rate                                    |
| `RM`      | predictor | Average rooms per dwelling                               |
| `DIS`     | predictor | Weighted distance to Boston employment centres           |
| `AGE`     | predictor | Share of units built before 1940                         |

#### Baseline bml: equal weights

The `bml` baseline uses the weighting `w ~ 1/n`, giving every neighbour equal weight. This is the conventional MMMM where the membership structure is there and variable weights are permitted but in this case, the weights are fixed.
To prepare the data for bml, we build the long-format data first, then we fit the model.

```{r}
#| code-fold: true

nb2df <- function(nb) {
  data.frame(
    tid    = rep(seq_along(nb), lengths(nb)),
    tid_nb = unlist(nb)
  )
}

boston_df <-
  nb2df(boston_nb) |>
  group_by(tid) |> mutate(n = n()) |> ungroup() |>
  inner_join(boston, by = "tid") |>
  inner_join(
    as.data.frame(boston) |>
      select(-CMEDV, -lnCMEDV, -geom) |>
      rename_with(~ paste0(.x, "_nb")),
    by = c("tid_nb" = "tid_nb")
  )
```

```{r}
mod_bml <-
  bml(
    lnCMEDV ~ NOX + CRIM + RM + DIS + AGE +
      mm(
        id   = id(tid_nb, tid),                       # member id, group id
        vars = bml::vars(NOX_nb + CRIM_nb + RM_nb + DIS_nb + AGE_nb),
        fn   = fn(w ~ 1/n, c = TRUE),                 # equal weights, normalised to sum 1
        RE   = TRUE                                    # include neighbour-level random effect
      ),
    family   = "Gaussian",
    n.iter   = 5000,
    n.burnin = 500,
    n.chains = 3,
    seed     = 42,
    data     = boston_df
  )

summary(mod_bml)
```

#### Parameterised weights: similarity across covariates

Equal weighting assumes every neighbour matters the same regardless of how similar it is to the focal tract.
A natural alternative is that tracts are more strongly influenced by neighbours that resemble them, a spatial homophily effect.
Rather than collapse similarity into a single number, we let the weight depend on the dissimilarity in each covariate separately, using the functional form from Rosche (2026):

$$
w_{ij} = \frac{1}{1 + (n_i - 1)\exp\!\bigl(-(b_0 + b_1 \cdot d^{\text{CRIM}}_{ij} + b_2 \cdot d^{\text{AGE}}_{ij})\bigr)}
$$

Each `d` is the standardised absolute difference between a focal tract and its neighbour on that covariate.
When all the `b`'s are zero this collapses to `1/n_i`, the equal-weight baseline.
A negative coefficient means neighbours that are similar on that covariate (low `d`) receive more weight; a positive coefficient means dissimilar neighbours dominate.
Letting the two dissimilarities enter on their own lets the model decide which kind of similarity drives the aggregation, instead of us imposing a single combined measure, for example by adding up the absolute differences.

```{r}
#| code-fold: true

boston_df2 <- boston_df |>
  mutate(
    d_CRIM = as.numeric(scale(log1p(abs(CRIM - CRIM_nb)))),   # log first: crime is right-skewed
    d_AGE  = as.numeric(scale(abs(AGE - AGE_nb)))
  )
```

```{r, warning = FALSE}

mod_bml_w <-
  bml(
    lnCMEDV ~ NOX + CRIM + RM + DIS + AGE +
      mm(
        id   = id(tid_nb, tid),
        vars = bml::vars(NOX_nb + CRIM_nb + RM_nb + DIS_nb + AGE_nb),
        # Weight function from Rosche (2026): nests 1/n when all b's are 0
        fn   = fn(w ~ 1 / (1 + (n - 1) * exp(-(b0 + b1 * d_CRIM + b2 * d_AGE))), c = TRUE),
        RE   = TRUE
      ),
    family   = "Gaussian",
    priors   = list("b.w.1 ~ dnorm(0, 1)"),   # weakly informative; prevents exp() overflow
    n.iter   = 5000,
    n.burnin = 500,
    n.chains = 3,
    seed     = 42,
    data     = boston_df2
  )

summary(mod_bml_w)
monetPlot(mod_bml_w, "b.w.1[2]", label = "Crime-similarity weight")
```

The crime coefficient `b.w.1[2]` is −1.78 (CI [−2.54, −1.18]), clearly negative, so neighbours with similar crime levels carry more weight.
The age coefficient `b.w.1[3]` is 0.73 (CI [0.10, 1.53]), pointing the other way, with neighbours with different age profiles carry more weight.
The model picks up two kinds of similarity at once, in opposite directions.

#### Do the weights actually vary with similarity?

A direct check to see this is by plotting the estimated weight each neighbour received against the crime dissimilarity between that neighbour and its focal tract. If crime homophily is driving the weights, low-`d_CRIM` pairs should cluster toward high weights and high-`d_CRIM` pairs toward low weights.

```{r, fig.width = 6, fig.height = 4}
#| code-fold: true

w_mat <- mod_bml_w$w[[1]]   # 506 x 15 matrix, NA padded

weight_df <- boston_df2 |>
  group_by(tid) |>
  mutate(pos = row_number()) |>
  ungroup() |>
  mutate(weight = w_mat[cbind(tid, pos)]) |>
  filter(!is.na(weight))

weight_long <- weight_df |>
  tidyr::pivot_longer(c(d_CRIM, d_AGE), names_to = "covariate", values_to = "dissimilarity")

ggplot(weight_long, aes(x = dissimilarity, y = weight)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.15, size = 0.8) +
  geom_smooth(method = "loess", se = FALSE, color = "#2c7bb6") +
  facet_wrap(~ covariate, scales = "free_x") +
  labs(x = "Dissimilarity", y = "Estimated neighbour weight") +
  theme_minimal()
```

The two panels show the two similarity effects taking effect in opposite directions. The weight falls as crime dissimilarity rises (the homophily the negative crime coefficient pointed to). Weight rises gently with age dissimilarity, matching the positive age coefficient. Each panel holds only a partial view, since every neighbour's weight reflects both dissimilarities at once, but the opposing slopes are visible.

#### Benchmark: spatial Durbin error model

The classical spatial benchmark for spatial modeling problems like this is the SDEM, which models a tract's outcome as a function of its own covariates, its neighbours' covariates, and a spatially correlated error. We can fit it as a yardstick to compare our models to.

```{r}
mod_sdem <- errorsarlm(
  lnCMEDV ~ NOX + CRIM + RM + DIS + AGE,
  data   = boston,
  listw  = boston_wlist,
  Durbin = ~ NOX + CRIM + RM + DIS + AGE   # include neighbour-covariate effects (WX)
)
summary(mod_sdem)
```

The own and neighbour coefficients line up with the equal-weight bml model, which is the reassurance we want, as the bml baseline lines up with the classical benchmark.
If equal-weight neighbour effects were all we wanted, `errorsarlm` would serve, and it would be faster.
What the parameterised weight function adds is the aggregation itself as something to estimate, which the SDEM cannot do.

#### Model comparison

`bml` ships with `tidy()` and `glance()` methods, so we can pull fit statistics straight into a table without writing our own helpers.

```{r}
#| code-fold: true
bind_rows(
  glance(mod_bml)   |> mutate(model = "Equal weights (1/n)"),
  glance(mod_bml_w) |> mutate(model = "Similarity weights")
) |>
  select(model, DIC)
```

The similarity-weighted model fits better (DIC 298.5 vs 308.5). Letting the weights depend on crime and age similarity improves on equal weighting.

### Example 3: Political parties and the survival of coalition governments {#e3}
The previous two examples asked how peers or neighbours combine to shape an outcome. This example asks a similar question about coalition politics: how do the parties in a coalition combine to determine whether the government survives?
Each coalition government is composed of multiple parties, and each party brings its own features like organisational traits, funding structure, and internal cohesion. The MMMM question is not just whether party characteristics matter, but how they aggregate from the party level up to the government level, and which parties have a greater effect.

#### The Data
The `coalgov` dataset ships with the package. Each row is one party's participation in one government: 2,077 party-government pairs spanning 628 governments and 312 parties across 29 countries.

```{r, warning=FALSE}
#| code-fold: true
library(bml)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)

data(coalgov)

#The weight function will use `prime`, but it is stored as `TRUE`/`FALSE`, and `fn()` does not coerce logicals inside the formula, so we cast it to an integer first.
coalgov$prime <- as.integer(coalgov$prime)

# Pre-compute a clean smallest-party indicator for the proportional-vs-pivotal model.
# Computing it here (rather than as (pseat == min(pseat)) inside fn()) breaks ties
# so exactly one party is flagged per coalition, and keeps the weight function stable.
coalgov <- coalgov |>
  group_by(gid) |>
  mutate(is_smallest = as.integer(pseat == min(pseat) & pid == pid[which.min(pseat)])) |>
  ungroup()
```

| Variable    | Level        | Meaning    |
|-------------|--------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `gid`       | id           | Government identifier (the group)                                    |
| `pid`       | id           | Party identifier (the member)                                        |
| `dur_wkb`   | outcome      | Government duration in days                                          |
| `event_wkb` | outcome      | 1 = government fell from conflict; 0 = still standing when observed  |
| `majority`  | government   | 1 = majority coalition, 0 = minority                                |
| `mwc`       | government   | 1 = minimal winning coalition, 0 = oversized                        |
| `n`         | government   | Number of parties in the coalition                                  |
| `finance`   | party        | Party's dependence on outside money rather than dues (standardised) |
| `prime`     | party        | TRUE if the party holds the prime ministership, FALSE otherwise     |

The outcome is the pair `(dur_wkb, event_wkb)`, which is how survival data is recorded: a duration, plus a flag for whether the duration ended in the event of interest (the government falling) or was merely the last point at which we observed a government still in office.

#### Equal-weight baseline
Here, every party contributes equally to the coalition's financial profile. This is the natrul starting point, where the assumption is that each party has an equal contribution towards the outcome.


```{r}
mod_eq <-
  bml(
    Surv(dur_wkb, event_wkb) ~ 1 + majority + mwc +
      mm(
        id   = id(pid, gid),
        vars = vars(finance),
        fn   = fn(w ~ 1/n, c = TRUE),
        RE   = TRUE
      ),
    family   = "Weibull",
    monitor  = TRUE,
    n.iter   = 5000,
    n.burnin = 500,
    n.thin   = 1,
    n.chains = 3,
    seed     = 1,
    data     = coalgov
  )

summary(mod_eq)
```

The finance coefficient is negative and away from zero, which means that coalitions whose parties depend more on outside money tend to fall sooner. The coefficient is on the log-survival-time scale, so negative means shorter expected duration.

Before testing any alternative weighting scheme, we can run two checks. First, how much of the variation in government survival originates at the party level versus the government or country level.


```{r}
varDecomp(mod_eq)
```

Here, we see that 37% of the variance in government survival sits at the party level. Governments that share parties are more similar to each other than governments that do not, beyond anything the covariates explain. This shows that the multiple-membership structure is not just adding complexity, since more than a third of what drives coalition survival is carried by the parties themselves.

Our second check is to see if the chains converge. We run the mcmcDiag()
```{r}
mcmcDiag(mod_eq, parameters = c("b.mm.1", "sigma.mm.1"))
```

All four diagnostics are clean.
The Gelman-Rubin statistic sits at around 1.00 for both parameters, well below the 1.1 threshold, confirming the three chains converged to the same posterior.
Geweke z-scores and Heidelberger-Welch (p-values above 0.05) show no evidence of non-stationarity, and autocorrelation at lag 50 is negligible, indicating the chains mixed well.

#### Letting weights vary based on the parties

Equal weighting assumes a small junior/smaller partners shape the coalition's financial profile exactly as much as the party holding the prime ministership. We can replace that assumption with an estimate. The weight function lets the PM party carry more or less weight, with the amount estimated from the data.

We frame this with mixing parameters, that can be used to dial between PM's party and other parties. At `b1 = 1`, all weight goes to the prime minister's party. At `b1 = 0`, we recover a baseline aggregation rule. There are two natural baselines to blend against, and they ask different questions, so we fit both and compare.

##### Prime minister's party against equal weights

$$
w_{ij} = b_1 \cdot \text{prime}_{ij} + (1 - b_1)\cdot \tfrac{1}{n_i}
$$

This first blend puts the PM party against equal weighting. At `b1 = 1`, PM's party carries all the weight. At `b1 = 0` it is the `1/n` baseline, so the parameter measures how far the data pull away from equal weights toward the prime minister's party.

```{r, warning = FALSE}
mod_pm_n <-
  bml(
    Surv(dur_wkb, event_wkb) ~ 1 + majority + mwc +
      mm(
        id   = id(pid, gid),
        vars = vars(finance),
        fn   = fn(w ~ b1 * prime + (1 - b1) * (1/n), c = TRUE),
        RE   = TRUE
      ),
    family   = "Weibull",
    priors   = list("b.w.1 ~ dnorm(0, 1)"),
    monitor  = TRUE,
    n.iter   = 5000,
    n.burnin = 500,
    n.thin   = 1,
    n.chains = 3,
    seed     = 1,
    data     = coalgov
  )

summary(mod_pm_n)
```

The weight parameter `b.w.1` (prime) is 0.21, with a 95% interval [-0.10, 0.52]. The estimate is positive, so the data leans towards the prime minister's party carrying more weight than equal aggregation would assign it, but the interval includes zero, so equal weighting cannot be ruled out. The finance effect is -0.27 [-0.52, -0.03], close to one in the baseline's -0.32 and still clearly negative, so the story that coalitions reliant on outside money fall sooner holds whether or not the PM party is up-weighted.

```{r}
monetPlot(mod_pm_n, "b.w.1", label = "b1: 1 = all weight on PM party, 0 = equal weights")
```

The posterior density is a single hump centred at about 0.21, just right of the solid zero line. Most of its mass lies to the right of zero, but a clear slice falls left of the line, zero stays inside the interval. The heavy horizontal bar marks the 95% interval and its left end sits left of zero. In the scan panel below, the three chains overlap and cover the same range with no separation.

```{r}
mcmcDiag(mod_pm_n, parameters = c("b.w.1", "b.mm.1"))
```

The diagnostics are clean. Gelman-Rubin is 1.01 for `b.w.1` and 1.00 for finance, both well under the 1.1 threshold. The Geweke z-scores (-1.08, -0.96) sit within the usual bounds, the Heidelberger-Welch p-values (0.42, 0.36) are above 0.05, and lag-50 autocorrelation is small (0.03, 0.07). The chains converged, are stationary, and mixed well.

##### Prime minister against seat share

$$
w_{ij} = b_1 \cdot \text{prime}_{ij} + (1 - b_1)\cdot \text{pseat}_{ij}
$$

This second blend includes the PM party against seat-share proportionality. At `b1 = 0` weights track each party's seat share, so here the parameter measures how far the data pull away from proportionality toward the prime minister's party.

```{r, warning = FALSE}
mod_pm_p <-
  bml(
    Surv(dur_wkb, event_wkb) ~ 1 + majority + mwc +
      mm(
        id   = id(pid, gid),
        vars = vars(finance),
        fn   = fn(w ~ b1 * prime + (1 - b1) * pseat, c = TRUE),
        RE   = TRUE
      ),
    family   = "Weibull",
    priors   = list("b.w.1 ~ dnorm(0, 1)"),
    monitor  = TRUE,
    n.iter   = 5000,
    n.burnin = 500,
    n.thin   = 1,
    n.chains = 3,
    seed     = 1,
    data     = coalgov
  )

summary(mod_pm_p)
```

Here,`b.w.1` (prime) is 0.12 with a 95% interval [-0.23, 0.53]. The estimate is again positive with the interval spanning zero. The smaller point estimate with the wider interval means the pull toward the PM party is even weaker here than in the first parameterization. The finance effect has changed to -0.21 [-0.45, 0.02], its interval now just touching zero. 

```{r}
monetPlot(mod_pm_p, "b.w.1", label = "b1: 1 = all weight on PM party, 0 = proportional to seats")
```

The density has the same shape but shifted closer to zero, centred at about 0.12. The scan panel again shows three well-mixed, overlapping chains.
```{r}
mcmcDiag(mod_pm_p, parameters = c("b.w.1", "b.mm.1"))
```

Convergence is sound. Gelman-Rubin 1.01 for `b.w.1` and for finance, Geweke z-scores near zero (-0.32, 0.38), Heidelberger-Welch p-values above 0.05 (0.37, 0.38), and negligible lag-50 autocorrelation. 


##### Comparing the models

```{r}
#| code-fold: true
# Finance and weight parameters across the baseline and both blends
bind_rows(
  tidy(mod_eq)   |> mutate(model = "Equal weights"),
  tidy(mod_pm_n) |> mutate(model = "PM vs equal (1/n)"),
  tidy(mod_pm_p) |> mutate(model = "PM vs seat share")
) |>
  filter(grepl("finance|b.w.1", term)) |>
  select(model, term, estimate, conf.low, conf.high)

# Model fit
bind_rows(
  glance(mod_eq)   |> mutate(model = "Equal weights (1/n)"),
  glance(mod_pm_n) |> mutate(model = "PM vs equal (1/n)"),
  glance(mod_pm_p) |> mutate(model = "PM vs seat share")
) |>
  select(model, DIC, nobs) |>
  arrange(DIC)
```

Across both versions the weight parameter is positive but its interval includes zero, so the prime minister's party does not measurably outweigh its partners in shaping the coalition's financial profile, based on the comparison against equal weights and against seat share. 
The finance effect weakens slightly across the table, from -0.32 at baseline to -0.27 and then -0.21, staying negative throughout except for the last version's interval that just touched zero. 
On fit, PM vs Equal Weights has the lowest DIC (3859 against the baseline's 3868), with PM vs Proportional in between (3863). The gap is small and it comes from a parameter we cannot distinguish from zero.




